<h2>The structure of Order 41 Rules 1 and 2</h2>
<p>Rule 1 makes the memorandum the charter of the appeal: grounds of objection, stated distinctly and numbered. Rule 2 has two limbs that pull in opposite directions and together define the court\'s flexibility:</p>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h4>The restraint</h4><p>The appellant shall not, except by leave of the court, urge or be heard in support of any ground of objection not set forth in the memorandum of appeal.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>The freedom</h4><p>The appellate court is not confined to the memorandum: it may decide the appeal on any ground — provided the affected party has had a sufficient opportunity to contest the case on that ground.</p></div>
</div>
<p>The proviso is the hinge. Whether the new point comes from counsel or from the bench, natural justice is the non-negotiable condition: the other side must get a real chance to meet it, by adjournment, written note or fresh hearing as the situation demands.</p>

<h2>When leave is realistically granted</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Nature of the new ground</th><th>Prospects</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Pure question of law on admitted or proved facts (limitation on the face of the plaint, statutory bar, jurisdiction)</td><td>Strong — such pleas go to the root and need no fresh evidence.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Legal effect of documents already exhibited</td><td>Good — construction of a deed or contract on record is a question of law.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Ground omitted by oversight but fully covered by the trial record and pleadings</td><td>Discretionary — courts weigh prejudice, delay and the interests of justice.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Plea requiring new facts or evidence (a new case of title, adverse possession not pleaded, fresh custom)</td><td>Weak — a party cannot make out a new case at the appellate stage.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Plea abandoned or conceded at trial</td><td>Weakest — conscious abandonment is treated differently from oversight.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The first appeal\'s special character</h2>
<p>These rules take their colour from what a first appeal under Section 96 CPC is: a re-hearing of the whole case on facts and law, in which the appellate court\'s duty — reflected in Order 41 Rule 31 — is to frame the points for determination and decide them with reasons. That width is why the leave power exists at all. It also explains the contrast with second appeals under Section 100, which live and die on substantial questions of law formulated by the High Court; there the framing is jurisdictional, and the room for improvisation correspondingly smaller.</p>

<h2>Practice notes</h2>
<div class="check">
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Draft the memorandum as if leave will be refused.</strong> Include every ground the record supports, in the alternative where necessary; leave is a safety net, not a drafting strategy.</li>
    <li><strong>Seek leave formally and early.</strong> An application identifying the new ground, explaining the omission and demonstrating that no new evidence is needed fares far better than a point sprung in oral arguments.</li>
    <li><strong>Pair the analysis with Rule 27 honestly.</strong> If the new ground needs new material, the real application is under Order 41 Rule 27, with its own stringent conditions — conflating the two annoys courts and loses both.</li>
    <li><strong>Respondents: insist on the proviso.</strong> If a new ground is permitted, ask for time and, where appropriate, for the opportunity to file a written response; prejudice conceded in haste cannot be complained of later.</li>
    <li><strong>Watch for the court\'s own grounds.</strong> Since the bench may decide on an unpleaded ground after hearing the parties, closing submissions should address the weaknesses of the decree, not merely the grounds exchanged.</li>
  </ul>
</div>
<p>The memorandum of appeal disciplines advocacy; it does not imprison justice. The art lies in drafting so completely that leave is never needed — and in knowing, when it is, how to ask for it so the court can say yes.</p>
<div class="note"><p>This article is for general information only and is not legal advice. Whether a particular plea can be raised on appeal depends on the pleadings, the trial record and the stage of the proceedings.</p></div>
